1033
01:01:39,578 --> 01:01:43,361
[SPEAKER_01]: We went to Days Inn, but they were all filled up and then derped us.

1034
01:01:46,513 --> 01:01:50,155
[SPEAKER_01]: going very early in the afternoon, no business for them to be filled up.

1035
01:01:50,195 --> 01:01:52,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Besides, today's was a budget motel.

1036
01:01:52,396 --> 01:01:54,157
[SPEAKER_01]: We thought we'd be able to save more money than that.

1037
01:01:55,438 --> 01:01:56,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we left there.

1038
01:01:57,039 --> 01:01:58,960
[SPEAKER_01]: It was getting hot, very warm.

1039
01:01:59,841 --> 01:02:03,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Went on our way back towards town a little bit.

1040
01:02:03,583 --> 01:02:07,665
[SPEAKER_01]: That stays in being 12, 15 miles outside of town on the east end.

1041
01:02:09,686 --> 01:02:15,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And we got into, found this place here, the Expressway Motel.

1042
01:02:17,288 --> 01:02:36,777
[SPEAKER_01]: About 41, which is maybe 7 or 8 miles, maybe not quite so far out from the main part of town, a little bit southeast by southeast, something like that.

1043
01:02:36,817 --> 01:02:45,701
[SPEAKER_01]: In the afternoon, we went later after we got cooled off and fed, we went into town to see the Grand, or the Country Music Hall of Fame.

1044
01:02:46,122 --> 01:02:57,987
[SPEAKER_01]: a big building and all kinds of exhibits and plaques, gold star records, clothing worn by the stars, and a country music hall of fame.

1045
01:02:59,428 --> 01:03:02,429
[SPEAKER_01]: It was amusing and entertaining, but I still can't believe it.

1046
01:03:02,809 --> 01:03:14,174
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know, right across the street from the place there, it's a vacant lot now at the east side, which says, there will rise here in the future, the gospel music hall of fame.

1047
01:03:14,535 --> 01:03:15,435
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think of that?

1048
01:03:16,150 --> 01:03:19,672
[SPEAKER_01]: They also have here a Nashville Songwriters Hall of Fame.

1049
01:03:22,954 --> 01:03:23,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Crazy.

1050
01:03:26,096 --> 01:03:26,936
[SPEAKER_01]: And we came back.

1051
01:03:26,996 --> 01:03:32,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And meanwhile, at the motel, I tried a couple of times again to call Lucas, and still no answer.

1052
01:03:33,600 --> 01:03:36,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So I began to think there's somebody else around Nashville.

1053
01:03:37,062 --> 01:03:43,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I had on my list, but I eliminated because I wanted Lucas and Gooch were the top ones.

1054
01:03:43,786 --> 01:03:45,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And I looked on the list, and there was Johnny Gill.

1055
01:03:47,924 --> 01:03:52,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And I checked over his address and found it wasn't too far away.

1056
01:03:52,488 --> 01:03:55,871
[SPEAKER_01]: So I called him up, and he was nice.

1057
01:03:55,891 --> 01:04:00,395
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a little hard of hearing, but he's, you know, he'd be willing to talk.

1058
01:04:00,415 --> 01:04:02,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So I planned to go out there about 7.30.

1059
01:04:02,797 --> 01:04:06,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So we went down at 8, around 6, and came back.

1060
01:04:06,901 --> 01:04:14,808
[SPEAKER_01]: And I left here to go over to Gil's house and got there about 7.30 in Sweetbriar, a little bit southwest of the center of town.

1061
01:04:16,460 --> 01:04:18,701
[SPEAKER_01]: nice residential neighborhood there, quiet.

1062
01:04:21,682 --> 01:04:22,902
[SPEAKER_01]: The old house.

1063
01:04:24,183 --> 01:04:25,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And he came out to greet me.

1064
01:04:26,424 --> 01:04:30,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So the interview ran for about an hour, I guess.

1065
01:04:30,705 --> 01:04:42,649
[SPEAKER_01]: The first half, the first slide, this tape fairly solid, but then it began to thin out on this side, as one can readily tell.

1066
01:04:43,250 --> 01:04:44,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me check this.

1067
01:04:48,876 --> 01:04:50,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

1068
01:04:52,137 --> 01:04:53,957
[SPEAKER_01]: We sat out on the back patio.

1069
01:04:54,378 --> 01:04:57,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It was daylight for about an hour.

1070
01:04:57,859 --> 01:05:00,559
[SPEAKER_01]: It began to get kind of dark a little later, so we turned the light on.

1071
01:05:09,502 --> 01:05:11,342
[SPEAKER_01]: We sat out on the back patio.

1072
01:05:11,742 --> 01:05:14,723
[SPEAKER_01]: It was daylight for about an hour.

1073
01:05:15,303 --> 01:05:17,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It began to get kind of dark a little later, so we turned the light on.

1074
01:05:18,986 --> 01:05:26,732
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a tall man, six foot, the encyclopedia says 6'2 and 190 pounds.

1075
01:05:27,913 --> 01:05:30,475
[SPEAKER_01]: He's tall, he doesn't look that heavy now.

1076
01:05:33,317 --> 01:05:35,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Gray hair, he has most of it.

1077
01:05:36,479 --> 01:05:47,808
[SPEAKER_01]: He reminded me a little bit of Randolph Downs, the historian at Toledo University, with his wavy dark hair and the shape of his face.

1078
01:05:49,617 --> 01:05:51,298
[SPEAKER_01]: and his manner a little bit.

1079
01:05:52,179 --> 01:06:00,604
[SPEAKER_01]: He was soft-spoken, pleasant drawl, was a little hard of hearing, not too articulate.

1080
01:06:01,725 --> 01:06:05,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Memory was a little hazy here and there.

1081
01:06:06,447 --> 01:06:07,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Didn't have too much to say.

1082
01:06:08,168 --> 01:06:10,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, this was a rather difficult interview.

1083
01:06:11,690 --> 01:06:17,614
[SPEAKER_01]: There was much more that I'm sure he could have said, but he just wasn't there.

1084
01:06:18,895 --> 01:06:20,216
[SPEAKER_01]: and easy conversations.

1085
01:06:20,256 --> 01:06:21,057
[SPEAKER_01]: He was very nice.

1086
01:06:21,077 --> 01:06:22,678
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a nice chat.

1087
01:06:23,279 --> 01:06:29,344
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was kind of hard getting things out of him, as you can readily see.

1088
01:06:30,325 --> 01:06:32,666
[SPEAKER_01]: He jumped around a little bit.

1089
01:06:33,027 --> 01:06:35,048
[SPEAKER_01]: It was kind of hard keeping things in track.

1090
01:06:35,068 --> 01:06:36,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And I finally gave up after a while.

1091
01:06:37,751 --> 01:06:40,313
[SPEAKER_01]: I just zeroed in on a few broad questions.

1092
01:06:41,934 --> 01:06:46,518
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't think he was too successful.

1093
01:06:48,962 --> 01:06:59,066
[SPEAKER_01]: would be a good one to use as a test for students to see how they would have handled some of these things.

1094
01:06:59,906 --> 01:07:01,327
[SPEAKER_01]: His wife was not there.

1095
01:07:01,527 --> 01:07:02,747
[SPEAKER_01]: The house was being torn up.

1096
01:07:02,787 --> 01:07:05,508
[SPEAKER_01]: He said he was painting and redecorating it later.

1097
01:07:05,729 --> 01:07:06,649
[SPEAKER_01]: It's certainly true.

1098
01:07:10,410 --> 01:07:12,011
[SPEAKER_01]: He signed the ball for me.

1099
01:07:12,111 --> 01:07:14,192
[SPEAKER_01]: After I asked about pictures, he went and came out

1100
01:07:16,515 --> 01:07:25,599
[SPEAKER_01]: a two-frame pictures in a joint frame, about 8 by 10 where the frame is hinged and you close it like a book.

1101
01:07:25,639 --> 01:07:29,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Two 8 by 10 pictures of himself as a Chicago Cub.

1102
01:07:30,581 --> 01:07:38,344
[SPEAKER_01]: There wasn't enough light outside, even though they had the overhead light on, so we went in and I took those with a 35 millimeter camera and they probably won't come out.

1103
01:07:39,124 --> 01:07:46,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I also took a picture of himself standing with an oil painting of him

1104
01:07:46,460 --> 01:07:47,360
[SPEAKER_01]: done by his wife.

1105
01:07:47,780 --> 01:07:49,961
[SPEAKER_01]: The place is filled with oil paintings, she does.

1106
01:07:51,081 --> 01:07:56,462
[SPEAKER_01]: This was a very nice recent portrait of himself sitting comfortably in a rocking chair.

1107
01:07:57,982 --> 01:08:01,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So I've got a picture of him holding that picture.

1108
01:08:02,183 --> 01:08:15,325
[SPEAKER_01]: He also signed for me and gave me three postcard-sized cards, much like those Jim Rowe cards that I have, two with the Cubs uniform.

1109
01:08:15,893 --> 01:08:16,753
[SPEAKER_01]: one in Washington.

1110
01:08:16,913 --> 01:08:18,554
[SPEAKER_01]: He sent those for me.

1111
01:08:20,315 --> 01:08:23,436
[SPEAKER_01]: He, as he indicated, he had some stuff packed away.

1112
01:08:23,456 --> 01:08:31,099
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't know where it was, though, so he wasn't much hope for that.

1113
01:08:32,640 --> 01:08:34,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Mrs. Gill, as I said, wasn't there.

1114
01:08:34,981 --> 01:08:39,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But after we went in, I asked him if she would come down.

1115
01:08:39,503 --> 01:08:40,263
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd like to meet her.

1116
01:08:41,003 --> 01:08:44,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So he called when she finally came down upstairs.

1117
01:08:44,385 --> 01:08:45,205
[SPEAKER_01]: She'd been upstairs.

1118
01:08:46,555 --> 01:08:47,516
[SPEAKER_01]: resting, I guess.

1119
01:08:48,756 --> 01:08:50,417
[SPEAKER_01]: She was a small woman.

1120
01:08:50,897 --> 01:08:57,101
[SPEAKER_01]: She had blonde hair and very pleasant.

1121
01:08:58,162 --> 01:08:59,562
[SPEAKER_01]: She's a Nashville gal.

1122
01:08:59,602 --> 01:09:06,786
[SPEAKER_01]: They have this very pleasant speaking pattern.

1123
01:09:06,847 --> 01:09:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Very nice, hospitable.

1124
01:09:11,269 --> 01:09:14,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And she knew my name and where she got it from.

1125
01:09:15,793 --> 01:09:17,994
[SPEAKER_01]: I just said it that once on the phone a second ago.

1126
01:09:18,014 --> 01:09:20,595
[SPEAKER_01]: She answered the phone, and I said, Jane Murdoch.

1127
01:09:21,515 --> 01:09:27,357
[SPEAKER_01]: The only time I said it, she picked it up because she came downstairs, Mr. Murdoch.

1128
01:09:28,858 --> 01:09:30,258
[SPEAKER_01]: So she remembered it.

1129
01:09:31,098 --> 01:09:33,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So I complimented her on her paintings.

1130
01:09:33,239 --> 01:09:34,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember that?

1131
01:09:36,941 --> 01:09:39,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not always the right thing to do, but they were nice paintings.

1132
01:09:41,202 --> 01:09:42,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So she came out in the back.

1133
01:09:42,342 --> 01:09:43,663
[SPEAKER_01]: She wanted to hear part of the tape.

1134
01:09:44,733 --> 01:09:48,054
[SPEAKER_01]: and I played about the last 10 minutes of the first side of this tape.

1135
01:09:48,554 --> 01:09:49,695
[SPEAKER_01]: She was quite interested in that.

1136
01:09:50,395 --> 01:09:54,076
[SPEAKER_01]: I began packing my gear away, and we talked generally.

1137
01:09:54,956 --> 01:10:10,001
[SPEAKER_01]: She would have been a good one to have in the interview, because she was quite a much freer talker, had more initiative in the conversation, and would have been able to fill in a lot of gaps and to spur his recollections.

1138
01:10:10,961 --> 01:10:12,241
[SPEAKER_01]: A couple of things she said.

1139
01:10:14,907 --> 01:10:18,968
[SPEAKER_01]: when the portion of the tape came up about his being thrown out of games.

1140
01:10:19,068 --> 01:10:22,728
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, oh, two or three, maybe a three or four standard.

1141
01:10:24,049 --> 01:10:33,950
[SPEAKER_01]: She said about the time the first game she went to, which he played, where he was thrown out of the game, she said, oh, she was so embarrassed.

1142
01:10:34,070 --> 01:10:35,511
[SPEAKER_01]: She thought this was dreadful.

1143
01:10:35,551 --> 01:10:36,611
[SPEAKER_01]: She wanted to hide.

1144
01:10:37,471 --> 01:10:40,552
[SPEAKER_01]: And then she found out it wasn't so unusual for him to be thrown out of the game.

1145
01:10:40,572 --> 01:10:42,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So she didn't think it was quite so bad.

1146
01:10:44,109 --> 01:10:45,710
[SPEAKER_01]: He was horrified that first time.

1147
01:10:46,231 --> 01:10:48,813
[SPEAKER_01]: She said she did go to a lot of the games.

1148
01:10:49,133 --> 01:10:56,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Joe, I didn't know much about baseball before, but I traveled occasionally with the club, I guess.

1149
01:10:56,879 --> 01:11:02,703
[SPEAKER_01]: They got married in 1932 when he was with Chattanooga, I believe.

1150
01:11:03,904 --> 01:11:05,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Although she was not from there.

1151
01:11:05,325 --> 01:11:07,327
[SPEAKER_01]: She's from Nashville.

1152
01:11:07,347 --> 01:11:09,649
[SPEAKER_01]: They have no children.

1153
01:11:11,090 --> 01:11:13,171
[SPEAKER_01]: He expressed great interest in the tape.

1154
01:11:14,278 --> 01:11:20,442
[SPEAKER_01]: I offered to make a copy and send it to her for that, and she didn't want me to.

1155
01:11:21,243 --> 01:11:27,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'll go out of my way to do this, but I volunteered to do it, so I will.

1156
01:11:27,066 --> 01:11:31,249
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't have a recorder, but I said, well, maybe something you know has one you can borrow.

1157
01:11:32,050 --> 01:11:33,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you're not really that expensive.

1158
01:11:33,831 --> 01:11:37,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe you want to have one at your home.

1159
01:11:37,533 --> 01:11:43,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So I guess this was a new experience to have, being on tape like this.

1160
01:11:46,003 --> 01:11:48,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, she was chatting about various things.

1161
01:11:49,326 --> 01:11:54,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And asked me about Warren Harding, if he wasn't from my town.

1162
01:11:54,390 --> 01:11:57,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, no, he's Marian, not Marietta.

1163
01:12:00,695 --> 01:12:01,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Chatted a little bit about Harding.

1164
01:12:02,036 --> 01:12:12,785
[SPEAKER_01]: I mentioned what a great baseball fan he was, how he would have the stars of the day come to the White House for dinner and chat with the president.

1165
01:12:15,684 --> 01:12:20,708
[SPEAKER_01]: We'd talk about all his years out in the Pacific Coast League.

1166
01:12:22,389 --> 01:12:27,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Lefty, she says, she asked, did he tell you about playing for Lefty O'Doul?

1167
01:12:28,454 --> 01:12:31,296
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, no, when I was out in San Francisco.

1168
01:12:31,376 --> 01:12:37,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said about that time, I said, I think I better put this tape back on and interview you, too.

1169
01:12:37,341 --> 01:12:40,003
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember a lot of these things.

1170
01:12:40,023 --> 01:12:42,685
[SPEAKER_01]: She chuckled and said, no, I don't remember much.

1171
01:12:45,421 --> 01:12:49,482
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll talk now a little bit.

1172
01:12:49,502 --> 01:12:51,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Fifteen, twenty minutes more out there.

1173
01:12:51,063 --> 01:12:54,344
[SPEAKER_01]: I was sipping the lemonade he brought, delicious glass of lemonade.

1174
01:12:54,764 --> 01:13:00,805
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, sorry I didn't have any more, but this is a teetotaling house.

1175
01:13:00,825 --> 01:13:03,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Which reminded me of something else that came up there.

1176
01:13:04,006 --> 01:13:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: How she said that something to this effect, Johnny's career would have been

1177
01:13:12,150 --> 01:13:17,633
[SPEAKER_01]: lasted much longer if he was such a playboy, something like that, which, of course, I didn't learn about.

1178
01:13:17,653 --> 01:13:18,353
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't mention it.

1179
01:13:19,514 --> 01:13:21,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, well, you're probably right.

1180
01:13:21,435 --> 01:13:26,337
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't like Kent Greenfield, he talked about earlier, who drank himself out of the big leagues.

1181
01:13:26,777 --> 01:13:28,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But I had my fun.

1182
01:13:30,179 --> 01:13:36,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So there's something there which, if I'd known about, I might have pursued a little bit had the tape been on.

1183
01:13:36,943 --> 01:13:41,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Apparently, he enjoyed himself in baseball, too.

1184
01:13:45,281 --> 01:14:03,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, invited me to come back any time and I said, maybe write them before coming down again and they can dig out some of the scrapbooks or whatever they do have packed away, which he doesn't know where it is right now, but they do have something.

1185
01:14:04,757 --> 01:14:13,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I mentioned a Minor League record book, which Saber's putting out, and what the idea that was, and that he would begin

1186
01:14:14,922 --> 01:14:21,865
[SPEAKER_01]: She mentioned that, Johnny, didn't you get something from somebody about your minor league record, trying to fill in some gaps?

1187
01:14:21,885 --> 01:14:23,986
[SPEAKER_01]: She says, I think that's what it was.

1188
01:14:24,006 --> 01:14:27,747
[SPEAKER_01]: They were putting together this book, and they wanted more information from you.

1189
01:14:27,767 --> 01:14:33,029
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd forgotten it, but this, remember, this recalled it to his mind.

1190
01:14:33,049 --> 01:14:34,410
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, yes, I think that's right.

1191
01:14:34,430 --> 01:14:39,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So I told him they would certainly get a copy when that came out.

1192
01:14:41,864 --> 01:14:46,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to make sure that happens if they don't get one.

1193
01:14:46,266 --> 01:14:53,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Through ordinary channels, we don't hear about it, that somebody does contact them.

1194
01:14:57,132 --> 01:14:58,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything else happen there?

1195
01:14:58,233 --> 01:15:08,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Those were the main things.

1196
01:15:08,298 --> 01:15:11,120
[SPEAKER_01]: He spoke about his artificial hip, which he had put in.

1197
01:15:12,450 --> 01:15:14,451
[SPEAKER_01]: He walks with something of a limp.

1198
01:15:16,092 --> 01:15:18,213
[SPEAKER_01]: But he can paint his house and be decorated.

1199
01:15:18,233 --> 01:15:19,553
[SPEAKER_01]: He must be in pretty good shape.

1200
01:15:26,176 --> 01:15:39,282
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't think of much more to say at this time, so I think I will sign off and perhaps use the rest of the tape for some comment on one of the other interviews we have come with.

1201
01:15:39,608 --> 01:15:57,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Tomorrow, on August 3rd, we're going to travel down to Alabama, perhaps go to the Huntsville Space Center, and then on to Tuscaloosa, where on Thursday, the 4th of August, I'm due to have the interview with Joey Sewell, and hopefully Rick Stevens.

1202
01:15:59,952 --> 01:16:03,033
[SPEAKER_01]: But I know Sewell's all right.

1203
01:16:03,473 --> 01:16:05,153
[SPEAKER_01]: I suggest Stevens will be, too.

1204
01:16:05,253 --> 01:16:06,493
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll have to wait and see.

1205
01:16:07,294 --> 01:16:09,474
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all for today, August 2nd.

1206
01:16:10,625 --> 01:16:11,227
[SPEAKER_01]: 1977.

1207
01:16:11,427 --> 01:16:13,211
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about 11.30 at night now.

1208
01:16:13,271 --> 01:16:19,303
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm signing off from the bathroom of our Expressway Motel in Nashville, Tennessee.

